export function renderGuide(heading) {
 document.querySelector('#main').innerHTML=heading('Your writing and reading practice.','Practise a skill, develop a paper, or make a reading note in your own words.','<a class="btn secondary" href="/api/guides/portable">Read the setup guide</a>')+`
 <div class="guide-area-grid"><section class="card"><span class="tag">LEARNING</span><h2>Practise and review</h2><ol><li>Start in <a href="#learning">Overview</a> to continue a module, or choose one under <a href="#courses">Courses</a>.</li><li>Use <a href="#practice">Practice</a> to find a difficulty, topic or skill. Hints and worked examples explain what the task expects.</li><li>Write an answer and choose <strong>Check answer</strong> or <strong>Save & get feedback</strong>. Use the feedback to revise one point.</li><li>Find earlier attempts and feedback in <a href="#writing">Saved answers</a>. <a href="#progress">Progress</a> keeps the learning history; course pages hold certificates.</li><li>Open <a href="#resources">Resources</a> for the academic phrasebook, research notes and exercise packs.</li></ol></section>
 <section class="card"><span class="tag">PAPERS</span><h2>Plan and write your paper</h2><ol><li>Open <a href="#papers">Papers</a> and connect the Obsidian vault on this computer. Existing projects appear as paper cards.</li><li>Create a paper or open an existing folder. Its root outline links sections, optional subsections and arguments. Bullet-only and empty arguments are welcome.</li><li>Open one argument. Read its purpose and neighbours, plan in <strong>Notes and bullet points</strong>, then write in <strong>Manuscript prose</strong>.</li><li>Keep sources and explanations in their separate fields. Your text, notes and reasoning save automatically to the vault.</li><li>Choose <strong>Review or ask about my text</strong> to work on a saved practice copy. After saving a revision there, use <strong>Save revised answer to my argument</strong> to return it explicitly.</li></ol></section></div>
 <section class="card guide-body"><h2>Reading, quotations and book chapters</h2><p>Open <a href="#reading">Reading &amp; notes</a> to create a paper summary, book overview or other reading document. You can start from your Obsidian literature cards and templates. Keep <strong>My summary</strong> separate from exact quotations with pages and context.</p><p>In a book overview, choose <strong>Add a chapter</strong> for each separate chapter document. Summaries and notes autosave to <strong>06_Academic_Writing_Lab/Reading</strong>. The local tutor gives tips on your own summary; without excerpts it cannot check fidelity to the source.</p><h2>Plan your next action on Papyr</h2><p>Choose <strong>Send to planner</strong>, enter an action and date, and optionally estimate Pomodoros. Tasks go into <strong>Papyr/Planner/YYYY-MM-DD.md</strong> in your vault. In focus mode this action is under <strong>Timer options / Notes &amp; finish</strong>.</p><p>Then run your existing Papyr USB or Wi-Fi sync from Obsidian on the Mac. Saving a task here does not transfer it to the reader. From Windows, let Obsidian Sync finish first.</p></section>
 <section class="card guide-body"><h2>Continue without losing your place</h2><p>Use <strong>Resume writing</strong> on a paper card or the saved activity link while a tutor is working. A review refers to its submitted version; you can keep revising. Existing exercise links and saved attempts remain available.</p><p>For short checked answers, press Enter to check and Enter again after a correct result to continue. In open writing, Enter adds a line and Command/Ctrl + Enter submits. Example answers unlock after two saved attempts; unrelated worked examples are available from the start.</p><p>Open writing can complete through a successful tutor criteria review or your own recorded review. Certificates describe completed practice, not an accredited proficiency level.</p></section>
 <section class="card guide-body"><h2>Focus and supporting tools</h2><p>Minimise the menu for more space. Use the red clock and <strong>Focus view</strong> while working on an exercise or argument. Timer completion does not submit your writing.</p><p><a href="#settings">Settings</a> holds the local tutor and backups. <a href="#obsidian">Obsidian & devices</a> holds companion practice and device setup. <a href="#comments">My questions & comments</a> keeps the notes you submit through <strong>Flag unclear wording</strong>.</p><p>Wait for a successful vault save and let Obsidian Sync finish before switching computers. Use <strong>Compare & recover</strong> when versions conflict. Saved practice answers and paper drafts remain distinct.</p></section>`;
}
